<header><p class="st-kicker">other · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Bailiwick of Guernsey</h1><p class="st-meta">1204 CE – present · capital Saint Peter Port</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing British Crown Dependency of Channel Islands, held by the crown as heir of the Dukes of Normandy since 1204.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Guernsey belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy</a> from the 10th century. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> lost continental Normandy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> in 1204, the islands stayed with the English crown, and a separate bailiwick, under its own bailiff and royal court, developed for Guernsey, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alderney">Alderney</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sark">Sark</a> and the smaller islands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Guernsey kept Norman customary law, its Royal Court of jurats and, later, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-deliberation">States of Deliberation</a> as its parliament. Its economy rested on fishing, knitting, privateering and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomatoes</a>, horticulture and finance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-peter-port">Saint Peter Port</a> was a major harbour. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> it sided with Parliament, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> lived there in exile from 1855.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Guernsey was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1940 to 1945. Today it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-dependencies">Crown Dependency</a>, not part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom</a>, with its own government, laws and taxes, while the UK handles defence and international relations.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bailiwick-of-guernsey.jpg" alt="Bailiwick of Guernsey" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guernsey.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1204 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint Peter Port</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity; Anglican, Methodist</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English; Guernesiais</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guernsey">Guernsey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guernsey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1615&amp;focus=bailiwick-of-guernsey" aria-label="Bailiwick of Guernsey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4621 454 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bailiwick of Guernsey lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4941 710L5025 711M4941 710L4970 663M4941 710L4997 651M4941 710L4946 711M4941 710L4949 703"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5025 711h0M4970 663h0M4997 651h0M4946 711h0M4949 703h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4941 710h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4941 710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saint Peter Port. <a href="/atlas?year=1615&amp;focus=bailiwick-of-guernsey">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
